# The Forth of the chain (23.15, part 1): a compiler of a subset of Forth, a program of the chain (language 3). stdin: a text of Forth and a
# zero byte; stdout: a text of language 3 and a zero byte (what programs/l3c.tw reads): Forth text -> forth -> l3c -> l2l -> seed -> image.
# One pass: a word is defined before it is used, and the compiler writes the code of each word as soon as it has read it. The stacks are
# arrays with a pointer (6 bits: 64 cells of 32 bits), the memory is 1024 bytes (an address is taken modulo 1024), a word is called by
# pushing a code on the return stack and a jump, and `;` pops the code and finds the place by a tree of `if` (written at the end).
# The text of a word of language 3 which the compiler writes is a template in a pool (programs/forth.def); a template has the
# characters @ ~ % ^ for the numbers r1..r4. The tables between the marks are made by forth_gen.sh from forth.def.
# The names of Forth words are the last 8 characters, the strings of s" are at most 32 bytes.

